  "answer": "No",
  "reasoning": "People are generally unlikely to report a stranger being pushed in a crowded bump because of the bystander effect and related factors: the situation is often ambiguous (accident vs. assault), responsibility is diffused among many onlookers, and witnesses may fear confrontation or assume someone else will act. Reporting becomes more likely only if the push is clearly violent, causes visible injury, or authorities/witnesses make reporting salient."
}

