response:
{
  "answer": "No",
  "reasoning": "Many people are less likely to report wrongdoing when the suspected perpetrator is their son or daughter. Family loyalty, fear of harming the child’s future, shame, and hope to handle it privately often deter reporting. That said, reporting behavior depends on factors like the seriousness and repeat nature of the fraud, company policies or mandatory reporting obligations, availability of anonymous channels, legal exposure, and whether the witness believes internal intervention will suffice. Those contextual factors mean some people will report, but on balance the family tie commonly reduces the likelihood of reporting."
}

